                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Confusing irradiation and contamination"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Forgetting to subtract background radiation"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mixing up ionising ability and penetrating ability"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Incorrect nuclear equation balancing"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Confusing fission and fusion"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Worked Example"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A comprehensive problem:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A radioactive source has an initial activity of 800 Bq. After 15 minutes, the activity (corrected for background) is 100 Bq."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "a) Calculate the half-life. b) How many half-lives until activity drops below 10 Bq? c) If this is a gamma source, describe two safety precautions when using it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Solution:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "a) 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 = 3 half-lives    Half-life = 15/3 = 5 minutes"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "b) 100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5 → 6.25 = 4 more half-lives    Total from start: 3 + 4 = 7 half-lives    Or calculate: need to reach <10 Bq from 800 Bq    800 × (1/2)^n < 10    n > 6.3, so n = 7 half-lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "c) Two safety precautions:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Store in a lead-lined container when not in use"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Handle with tongs to maintain distance from the body (Also acceptable: limit time of exposure, wear film badge, work behind lead screen)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "quizEmbed",
        "attrs": {
          "quizId": "check-nuclear-energy-summary-2"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Exam Tip: ",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "In exam questions, always use precise scientific language. For example, say 'the nucleus emits an alpha particle' not 'the atom gives off radiation'. Describe decay as 'random and spontaneous'. State that half-life is constant for a given isotope. These precise terms earn marks."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Key Terms Glossary"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "codeBlock",
        "attrs": {
          "language": "text"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| Isotope | Atoms with same protons, different neutrons |\n| Half-life | Time for half the nuclei to decay |\n| Activity | Decays per second (Bq) |\n| Background radiation | Radiation always present in environment |\n| Irradiation | Exposure to external radiation source |\n| Contamination | Radioactive material on/in the body |\n| Fission | Splitting of heavy nucleus |\n| Fusion | Joining of light nuclei |\n| Chain reaction | Self-sustaining nuclear reaction |\n| Critical mass | Minimum mass for chain reaction |\n| Moderator | Slows neutrons in reactor |\n| Control rod | Absorbs neutrons to control reaction |"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Practice Problems"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "State the nuclear equation for the alpha decay of uranium-235."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Calculate the fraction of a radioactive sample remaining after 8 hours if the half-life is 2 hours."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Explain why gamma sources are suitable for medical imaging while alpha sources are suitable for smoke detectors."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Compare nuclear fission and nuclear fusion in terms of process, fuel, conditions, and waste products."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Describe the function of control rods and the function of moderators in a nuclear reactor."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Calculate the activity of a Tc-99m dose (half-life 6 hours) after 24 hours, given an initial activity of 400 MBq."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Explain why achieving fusion on Earth requires higher temperatures than those found in the Sun's core."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockquote",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Remember: ",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "bold"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The key relationships to remember for this unit: ionising power and penetrating power are inversely related; mass number and atomic number are always conserved in nuclear equations; half-life is constant and unaffected by external conditions; and E = mc squared explains the enormous energy released in nuclear reactions."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Summary"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Atoms contain protons, neutrons (nucleus) and electrons"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Radioactive decay: α (loses 2p, 2n), β (n→p), γ (energy only)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Half-life: time for activity to halve, constant for each isotope"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Uses: medicine (imaging, treatment), industry (gauges, detectors)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Safety: time, distance, shielding; ALARA principle"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fission: splitting large nuclei, used in power stations"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fusion: joining small nuclei, powers stars, future energy source"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "E = mc² explains the enormous energy in nuclear reactions"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Answer Key"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "$^{235}_{92}U \\rightarrow ^{231}_{90}Th + ^4_2\\alpha$. A: 235 = 231 + 4; Z: 92 = 90 + 2.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — alpha decay reduces A by 4 and Z by 2 (calculation)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Number of half-lives = 8/2 = 4. Fraction remaining = (1/2)^4 = 1/16.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — apply halving formula with n = t/t_half (calculation)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Gamma sources for imaging: gamma rays penetrate through the body and can be detected externally; they allow doctors to see how organs function without surgery. Alpha sources for smoke detectors: alpha particles strongly ionise air to maintain a current; smoke absorbs alpha particles easily, reducing the current and triggering the alarm. Alpha cannot penetrate the detector casing, making it safe for home use.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — each radiation type matched to its application by its properties (reasoning)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fission: splits large nuclei (U-235, Pu-239) using neutron bombardment at modest temperatures; produces long-lived radioactive waste; chain reactions possible; used in current power stations. Fusion: joins light nuclei (H-2, H-3) requiring extreme temperatures (~100 million degrees C); main product is helium (non-radioactive); no chain reaction risk; still in research stage. Both release energy via E = mc^2.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — systematic comparison across four criteria (comparison)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Control rods (boron/cadmium) absorb excess neutrons; inserting them further reduces the reaction rate, withdrawing them increases it, maintaining a steady critical state. Moderator (water/graphite) slows fast neutrons to thermal speeds; slow neutrons are more likely to be absorbed by U-235 and cause fission, making the chain reaction more efficient.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — absorption vs slowing: complementary roles (description)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Number of half-lives = 24/6 = 4. Activity = 400 x (1/2)^4 = 400 x 1/16 = 25 MBq.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — straightforward halving calculation (calculation)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The Sun compensates for its lower core temperature (~15 million degrees C) with enormous gravitational pressure and density, which increases the probability of nuclei colliding. On Earth, we cannot replicate such extreme pressure and density, so the temperature must be much higher (~100 million degrees C) to give nuclei enough kinetic energy to overcome electrostatic repulsion and fuse.",
                    "marks": [
                      {
                        "type": "bold"
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " — lower density on Earth must be offset by higher temperature (reasoning)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}